{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":108340,"items":[{"DOI":"10.1080\/00150517.1984.12429896","title":["Coaxal Circles Associated with Recurrence-Generated Sequences"],"author":[{"given":"A. F.","family":"Horadam","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of New England","place":["Armidale, Australia"]}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[1984,8]]},"container-title":["The Fibonacci Quarterly"],"type":"journal-article","is-referenced-by-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1080\/00150517.1984.12429896"},{"DOI":"10.1090\/surv\/104\/06","title":["Arithmetic structure of recurrence sequences"],"author":[{"given":"Graham","family":"Everest","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Alf","family":"van der Poorten","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Igor","family":"Shparlinski","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Thomas","family":"Ward","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2003,7,1]]},"container-title":["Mathematical Surveys and Monographs","Recurrence Sequences"],"type":"book-chapter","is-referenced-by-count":4,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1090\/surv\/104\/06"},{"abstract":"<jats:p>In this article, we study a novel extension of the classic balancing numbers, referred to as the higher-order balancing numbers and denoted by. This sequence is analogous to the higher-order Fibonacci numbers and follows the same recurrence relation as the balancing sequence itself. The case k=1 gives the classic balancing numbers (A001109) and for k=2 gives the sequence A029547, thus establishing a direct link to existing number sequences. Here, we first establish the Binet-like formula and then, with its help, present various algebraic properties of this newly introduced sequence, such as recurrence relations, generating functions (both ordinary and exponential), partial sums, binomial sums, combined identities, and more. We also obtain the limiting ratio and establish several well-known identities, including Catalan\u2019s identity, d\u2019Ocagane\u2019s identity, Vajda\u2019s identity, Honsberger\u2019s identity, using the Binet-like formula. Finally, we give some mixed identity and series sum formulae. In this study, the obtained identities and algebraic properties are expressed in terms of the existing balancing and Lucas-balancing numbers.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.31489\/2026m1\/202-216","type":"journal-article","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Higher-order balancing numbers: new sequences, recurrence relations, generating functions and identities"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3653-5854","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"K.","family":"Prasad","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Indubala","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6541-0284","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"M.","family":"Kumari","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"container-title":["BULLETIN OF THE KARAGANDA UNIVERSITY-MATHEMATICS"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,30]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.31489\/2026m1\/202-216"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/cbo9780511566042.006","title":["Recurrence sequences"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[1986,11,27]]},"container-title":["Exponential Diophantine Equations"],"type":"book-chapter","is-referenced-by-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/cbo9780511566042.006"},{"DOI":"10.1090\/surv\/104\/04","title":["Operations on power series and linear recurrence sequences"],"author":[{"given":"Graham","family":"Everest","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Alf","family":"van der Poorten","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Igor","family":"Shparlinski","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Thomas","family":"Ward","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2003,7,1]]},"container-title":["Mathematical Surveys and Monographs","Recurrence Sequences"],"type":"book-chapter","is-referenced-by-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1090\/surv\/104\/04"},{"DOI":"10.1016\/0022-314x(87)90071-0","title":["Pure powers in recurrence sequences and some related diophantine equations"],"author":[{"given":"T.N.","family":"Shorey","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"C.L.","family":"Stewart","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[1987,11]]},"container-title":["Journal of Number Theory"],"type":"journal-article","is-referenced-by-count":21,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/0022-314x(87)90071-0"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/cbo9781139017732.018","title":["Recurrence sequences"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2012,7,5]]},"container-title":["Distribution Modulo One and Diophantine Approximation"],"type":"other","is-referenced-by-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/cbo9781139017732.018"},{"abstract":"<p>\n                    The Ap\u00e9ry numbers may be defined by a cubic three-term recurrence relation, that is, a three-term relation where the coefficients are polynomials in the index of degree\u00a0\n                    <inline-formula content-type=\"math\/mathml\">\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\" alttext=\"3\">\n                        <mml:semantics>\n                          <mml:mn>3<\/mml:mn>\n                          <mml:annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">3<\/mml:annotation>\n                        <\/mml:semantics>\n                      <\/mml:math>\n                    <\/inline-formula>\n                    .\n                  <\/p>\n                  <p>\n                    In this work, we first provide a systematic review of Ap\u00e9ry numbers and other related sequences that satisfy quadratic or cubic three-term recurrence relations, and show how they are interrelated and how they may be classified. This leads to sequences defined by cubic\n                    <inline-formula content-type=\"math\/mathml\">\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\" alttext=\"k\">\n                        <mml:semantics>\n                          <mml:mi>k<\/mml:mi>\n                          <mml:annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">k<\/mml:annotation>\n                        <\/mml:semantics>\n                      <\/mml:math>\n                    <\/inline-formula>\n                    -term recurrence relations. The cases corresponding to\n                    <inline-formula content-type=\"math\/mathml\">\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\" alttext=\"k equals 2\">\n                        <mml:semantics>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mi>k<\/mml:mi>\n                            <mml:mo>=<\/mml:mo>\n                            <mml:mn>2<\/mml:mn>\n                          <\/mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">k=2<\/mml:annotation>\n                        <\/mml:semantics>\n                      <\/mml:math>\n                    <\/inline-formula>\n                    in this framework lead to Ramanujan\u2019s theories of elliptic functions to alternative bases, while the cases corresponding to\n                    <inline-formula content-type=\"math\/mathml\">\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\" alttext=\"k equals 3\">\n                        <mml:semantics>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mi>k<\/mml:mi>\n                            <mml:mo>=<\/mml:mo>\n                            <mml:mn>3<\/mml:mn>\n                          <\/mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">k=3<\/mml:annotation>\n                        <\/mml:semantics>\n                      <\/mml:math>\n                    <\/inline-formula>\n                    correspond to the Ap\u00e9ry, Domb, Almkvist\u2013Zudilin numbers and other sequences that are well-studied.\n                  <\/p>\n                  <p>\n                    We conduct a detailed analysis for the case\n                    <inline-formula content-type=\"math\/mathml\">\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\" alttext=\"k equals 4\">\n                        <mml:semantics>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mi>k<\/mml:mi>\n                            <mml:mo>=<\/mml:mo>\n                            <mml:mn>4<\/mml:mn>\n                          <\/mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">k=4<\/mml:annotation>\n                        <\/mml:semantics>\n                      <\/mml:math>\n                    <\/inline-formula>\n                    . Some of the sequences that arise are new. Of particular interest are ten sequences that are said to be self-starting in the sense that a single initial condition is enough to start the recurrence relation. Of additional interest are two sequences which take values in\u00a0\n                    <inline-formula content-type=\"math\/mathml\">\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\" alttext=\"doubl